window.ETEXTBOOK={"units": [{"id": "u1", "title": "章节衔接导语", "sections": [{"id": "u1_s0", "title": "正文", "paras": [{"id": "u1_s0_p1", "buf": ["Culturally, indigenous (本土的) traditions, such as Confucianism and Daoism, along with the religions of foreign origin, such as Buddhism, have profoundly influenced the philosophy, literature, and arts of the Chinese people over the centuries. These traditions continue to demonstrate their enduring influence, showing their lasting charm for thousands of years."], "text": "Culturally, indigenous (本土的) traditions, such as Confucianism and Daoism, along with the religions of foreign origin, such as Buddhism, have profoundly influenced the philosophy, literature, and arts of the Chinese people over the centuries. These traditions continue to demonstrate their enduring influence, showing their lasting charm for thousands of years."}]}]}, {"id": "u2", "title": "Philosophy 哲学", "sections": [{"id": "u2_s1", "title": "引言与\"百家争鸣\"", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s1_p1", "buf": ["Chinese philosophy is the collective designation (总称) for various schools of thought. It developed on its own foundations with unique conceptual systems and methods of expression, while remaining open to ideas from other civilisations. Chinese philosophy is one of the three major philosophical traditions in the world, with the other two being Western philosophy and Indian philosophy."], "text": "Chinese philosophy is the collective designation (总称) for various schools of thought. It developed on its own foundations with unique conceptual systems and methods of expression, while remaining open to ideas from other civilisations. Chinese philosophy is one of the three major philosophical traditions in the world, with the other two being Western philosophy and Indian philosophy."}, {"id": "u2_s1_p2", "buf": ["Chinese philosophy is the essence of traditional Chinese culture and has evolved over several thousand years. Its origins can be traced back to the Xia, Shang, and Zhou dynasties. It began to take definite shape during the Spring and Autumn Period and flourished with the emergence of \"the contention of a hundred schools of thought\"."], "text": "Chinese philosophy is the essence of traditional Chinese culture and has evolved over several thousand years. Its origins can be traced back to the Xia, Shang, and Zhou dynasties. It began to take definite shape during the Spring and Autumn Period and flourished with the emergence of \"the contention of a hundred schools of thought\"."}, {"id": "u2_s1_p3", "buf": ["The contention of a hundred schools of thought (百家争鸣): The Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period were marked by profound social change and remarkable intellectual and philosophical vibrancy. During this time, various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Legalism, Daoism, and Mohism, emerged. Scholars wrote, lectured, and debated vigorously, creating a lively academic environment later described as \"the contention of a hundred schools of thought\".（春秋时期和战国时期，儒家、法家、道家和墨家等学派相继出现。学者们积极著书立说、讲学辩论，营造出一种活跃的学术氛围，后来被称为\"百家争鸣\"。）"], "text": "The contention of a hundred schools of thought (百家争鸣): The Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period were marked by profound social change and remarkable intellectual and philosophical vibrancy. During this time, various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Legalism, Daoism, and Mohism, emerged. Scholars wrote, lectured, and debated vigorously, creating a lively academic environment later described as \"the contention of a hundred schools of thought\".（春秋时期和战国时期，儒家、法家、道家和墨家等学派相继出现。学者们积极著书立说、讲学辩论，营造出一种活跃的学术氛围，后来被称为\"百家争鸣\"。）"}]}, {"id": "u2_s2", "title": "古代哲学的发展 Development of Ancient Chinese Philosophy", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s2_p1", "buf": ["In general, ancient Chinese philosophy progressed through the following periods."], "text": "In general, ancient Chinese philosophy progressed through the following periods."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p2", "buf": ["先秦子学 Philosophy in the Pre-Qin Period", "Philosophy in the pre-Qin period was characterised by the emergence of various ancient philosophical schools of thought. The most influential among them were Confucianism, Daoism, Mohism, and Legalism."], "text": "先秦子学 Philosophy in the Pre-Qin Period\nPhilosophy in the pre-Qin period was characterised by the emergence of various ancient philosophical schools of thought. The most influential among them were Confucianism, Daoism, Mohism, and Legalism."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p3", "buf": ["〔学科注〕Confucianism（儒家）: a school of thought represented by Confucius and Mencius, rooted in the teachings of Confucius, which serve as guiding codes (准则) for behaviour."], "text": "〔学科注〕Confucianism（儒家）: a school of thought represented by Confucius and Mencius, rooted in the teachings of Confucius, which serve as guiding codes (准则) for behaviour."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p4", "buf": ["Confucianism is a school of thought represented by Confucius and Mencius. It is rooted in the teachings of Confucius, which serve as guiding codes (准则) for behaviour. The school advocates core values such as benevolence (仁), righteousness (义), loyalty (忠), empathy (恕), and the Golden Mean. It values the ethical (伦理的) relationships among people. It emphasises the importance of family and societal order.〔续 19→20〕For example, children are expected to respect and care for their parents, the younger should defer to the elder, juniors should follow seniors, and individuals in subordinate roles are expected to fulfil their duties. The influence of Confucianism is so deep-rooted that it is often regarded as central to traditional Chinese life and culture. Confucianism is not confined to (局限于) China. Neighbouring countries such as Japan and Vietnam also embrace (信奉) Confucianism in their national life and culture."], "text": "Confucianism is a school of thought represented by Confucius and Mencius. It is rooted in the teachings of Confucius, which serve as guiding codes (准则) for behaviour. The school advocates core values such as benevolence (仁), righteousness (义), loyalty (忠), empathy (恕), and the Golden Mean. It values the ethical (伦理的) relationships among people. It emphasises the importance of family and societal order.〔续 19→20〕For example, children are expected to respect and care for their parents, the younger should defer to the elder, juniors should follow seniors, and individuals in subordinate roles are expected to fulfil their duties. The influence of Confucianism is so deep-rooted that it is often regarded as central to traditional Chinese life and culture. Confucianism is not confined to (局限于) China. Neighbouring countries such as Japan and Vietnam also embrace (信奉) Confucianism in their national life and culture."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p5", "buf": ["〔注〕the Golden Mean（中庸之道）: a major concept of the Confucian school. According to Confucius, the \"mean\" does not imply \"compromise\" but rather a \"moderate\" and \"just right\" approach to understanding and handling objective things.（\"中庸之道\"是儒家学派的一种理念，强调以\"适度\"且\"恰到好处\"的方式理解和处理客观事物。）"], "text": "〔注〕the Golden Mean（中庸之道）: a major concept of the Confucian school. According to Confucius, the \"mean\" does not imply \"compromise\" but rather a \"moderate\" and \"just right\" approach to understanding and handling objective things.（\"中庸之道\"是儒家学派的一种理念，强调以\"适度\"且\"恰到好处\"的方式理解和处理客观事物。）"}, {"id": "u2_s2_p6", "buf": ["Daoism (道家) is a school of thought that draws from the teachings of Laozi and Zhuangzi. The school advocates the doctrine that dao (道) is the course, the principle, the substance, and the standard of all things, to which all people should conform (遵从). Based on the work of Daodejing (also known as Laozi), Daoism promotes the belief that a person should live a simple life, avoiding excessive pursuit of wealth, fame, or power. Daoists believe that overindulgence (过度沉溺) in these pursuits can bring worries and troubles. With proper behaviour and self-restraint, a person can achieve great inner strength and lead a healthier, more balanced life. Daoism also promotes the political principle of \"achieving good governance through non-action\".【图注：Laozi（老子）】"], "text": "Daoism (道家) is a school of thought that draws from the teachings of Laozi and Zhuangzi. The school advocates the doctrine that dao (道) is the course, the principle, the substance, and the standard of all things, to which all people should conform (遵从). Based on the work of Daodejing (also known as Laozi), Daoism promotes the belief that a person should live a simple life, avoiding excessive pursuit of wealth, fame, or power. Daoists believe that overindulgence (过度沉溺) in these pursuits can bring worries and troubles. With proper behaviour and self-restraint, a person can achieve great inner strength and lead a healthier, more balanced life. Daoism also promotes the political principle of \"achieving good governance through non-action\".【图注：Laozi（老子）】"}, {"id": "u2_s2_p7", "buf": ["〔注〕non-action（无为）: letting things take their natural course and refraining from forced or unnatural interference. It is a basic concept of Daoism, representing an attitude towards the world and a political ideology held by ancient Daoists. It also emphasises respecting the natural laws rather than engaging in unnecessary or excessive actions.（\"无为\"是道家的基本理念，代表了古代道家对世界的态度以及一种政治思想。它强调尊重自然法则，避免强行干预或违背自然规律的行为。）"], "text": "〔注〕non-action（无为）: letting things take their natural course and refraining from forced or unnatural interference. It is a basic concept of Daoism, representing an attitude towards the world and a political ideology held by ancient Daoists. It also emphasises respecting the natural laws rather than engaging in unnecessary or excessive actions.（\"无为\"是道家的基本理念，代表了古代道家对世界的态度以及一种政治思想。它强调尊重自然法则，避免强行干预或违背自然规律的行为。）"}, {"id": "u2_s2_p8", "buf": ["Mohism (墨家), based on the teachings of Mozi, emphasises universal love (兼爱). This principle encourages people to love others, including their families and people of other states, as they love themselves, so that all may be treated equally. In politics and ethics, Mohism advocates honouring virtuous people, rejecting fatalism (宿命论) and aggressive wars, and promoting thriftiness (节俭) and simple funerals."], "text": "Mohism (墨家), based on the teachings of Mozi, emphasises universal love (兼爱). This principle encourages people to love others, including their families and people of other states, as they love themselves, so that all may be treated equally. In politics and ethics, Mohism advocates honouring virtuous people, rejecting fatalism (宿命论) and aggressive wars, and promoting thriftiness (节俭) and simple funerals."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p9", "buf": ["Legalism (法家), with Hanfeizi as one of its important representatives, believes that it is necessary to use strict laws to keep the state in order, to promote agricultural production, and to establish absolute and central power in the hands of the ruler.〔续 20→21〕In terms of society and history, Legalists believe that society develops and changes over time, which is considered a theoretical foundation for their advocacy of reform and innovation."], "text": "Legalism (法家), with Hanfeizi as one of its important representatives, believes that it is necessary to use strict laws to keep the state in order, to promote agricultural production, and to establish absolute and central power in the hands of the ruler.〔续 20→21〕In terms of society and history, Legalists believe that society develops and changes over time, which is considered a theoretical foundation for their advocacy of reform and innovation."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p10", "buf": ["两汉经学 Study of Confucian Classics During the Han Dynasty", "After the fall of the Qin Dynasty, it became clear that harsh laws alone could not sustain effective governance. In the Western Han Dynasty, Emperor Wudi adopted Dong Zhongshu's proposal of \"rejecting the other schools of thought and respecting only Confucianism (罢黜百家，独尊儒术)\". Consequently, Confucianism rose from among many schools of thought to become the official ideology."], "text": "两汉经学 Study of Confucian Classics During the Han Dynasty\nAfter the fall of the Qin Dynasty, it became clear that harsh laws alone could not sustain effective governance. In the Western Han Dynasty, Emperor Wudi adopted Dong Zhongshu's proposal of \"rejecting the other schools of thought and respecting only Confucianism (罢黜百家，独尊儒术)\". Consequently, Confucianism rose from among many schools of thought to become the official ideology."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p11", "buf": ["A major feature of this transformation was the study of Confucian classics. Scholars drew on pre-Qin Confucian classics and expressed their views of Confucian classics in the official script of the Han Dynasty (今文经学), which emphasised political relevance, and the study of classics in earlier script forms (古文经学), which focused on textual analysis. The Western Han favoured the former, while the Eastern Han saw the rise of the latter."], "text": "A major feature of this transformation was the study of Confucian classics. Scholars drew on pre-Qin Confucian classics and expressed their views of Confucian classics in the official script of the Han Dynasty (今文经学), which emphasised political relevance, and the study of classics in earlier script forms (古文经学), which focused on textual analysis. The Western Han favoured the former, while the Eastern Han saw the rise of the latter."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p12", "buf": ["魏晋玄学 Metaphysics in the Wei and Jin Dynasties", "Metaphysics in China mainly integrates the thoughts of Confucianism and Daoism. It is based on the famous classical works of The Book of Changes (also known as Yijing), Laozi, and Zhuangzi, collectively known as the Three Metaphysical Classics (三玄). To explore the noumenon (本体) of the universe was the theme of metaphysics in this period. It addressed key philosophical propositions, including \"existence versus non-existence\", \"words versus ideas\", and \"ethics versus nature\"."], "text": "魏晋玄学 Metaphysics in the Wei and Jin Dynasties\nMetaphysics in China mainly integrates the thoughts of Confucianism and Daoism. It is based on the famous classical works of The Book of Changes (also known as Yijing), Laozi, and Zhuangzi, collectively known as the Three Metaphysical Classics (三玄). To explore the noumenon (本体) of the universe was the theme of metaphysics in this period. It addressed key philosophical propositions, including \"existence versus non-existence\", \"words versus ideas\", and \"ethics versus nature\"."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p13", "buf": ["隋唐佛学 Buddhist Philosophy in the Sui and Tang Dynasties", "During the Sui and Tang dynasties, Buddhism reached its apex (顶点) in China. The Buddhist philosophy became integrated into the wisdom of Chinese philosophy. It mainly discussed the relationship between the subjective and the objective. By analysing spiritual phenomena and human rationality, Buddhist scholars sought to gain insight into the universe and human life."], "text": "隋唐佛学 Buddhist Philosophy in the Sui and Tang Dynasties\nDuring the Sui and Tang dynasties, Buddhism reached its apex (顶点) in China. The Buddhist philosophy became integrated into the wisdom of Chinese philosophy. It mainly discussed the relationship between the subjective and the objective. By analysing spiritual phenomena and human rationality, Buddhist scholars sought to gain insight into the universe and human life."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p14", "buf": ["宋明理学 Neo-Confucianism in the Song and Ming Dynasties", "During the Song and Ming dynasties, Neo-Confucianism developed into a prominent philosophical tradition. It integrated elements of Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism, and focused on the relationships among human beings, between human beings and nature, and between human beings and society, as well as on the nature of human beings."], "text": "宋明理学 Neo-Confucianism in the Song and Ming Dynasties\nDuring the Song and Ming dynasties, Neo-Confucianism developed into a prominent philosophical tradition. It integrated elements of Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism, and focused on the relationships among human beings, between human beings and nature, and between human beings and society, as well as on the nature of human beings."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p15", "buf": ["Neo-Confucianism has two main schools: the School of Principle (理学) and the School of Mind (心学). The School of Principle was represented by Zhou Dunyi, Cheng Hao, Cheng Yi, and Zhu Xi, who argued that \"rationalism\" is eternal and exists as a spiritual essence prior to the material world. They believed that all things in the universe are derived from \"rationalism\". The School of Mind was represented by Lu Jiuyuan and Wang Shouren, who advocated that \"there is nothing outside the mind and no rationalism (or reason) outside the mind\". They argued that subjective consciousness was the origin of all things in the world."], "text": "Neo-Confucianism has two main schools: the School of Principle (理学) and the School of Mind (心学). The School of Principle was represented by Zhou Dunyi, Cheng Hao, Cheng Yi, and Zhu Xi, who argued that \"rationalism\" is eternal and exists as a spiritual essence prior to the material world. They believed that all things in the universe are derived from \"rationalism\". The School of Mind was represented by Lu Jiuyuan and Wang Shouren, who advocated that \"there is nothing outside the mind and no rationalism (or reason) outside the mind\". They argued that subjective consciousness was the origin of all things in the world."}, {"id": "u2_s2_p16", "buf": ["明清实学 Philosophy of Application in the Ming and Qing Dynasties", "During the Ming and Qing dynasties, the school of application philosophy emphasised the promotion of practicality and the rejection of abstraction. It also advocated the rational utilisation of various academic disciplines, and held that academic studies should be beneficial for governance and administration, thereby realising their practical value."], "text": "明清实学 Philosophy of Application in the Ming and Qing Dynasties\nDuring the Ming and Qing dynasties, the school of application philosophy emphasised the promotion of practicality and the rejection of abstraction. It also advocated the rational utilisation of various academic disciplines, and held that academic studies should be beneficial for governance and administration, thereby realising their practical value."}]}, {"id": "u2_s3", "title": "古代中国哲学的基本特征 Basic Features of Ancient Chinese Philosophy", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s3_p1", "buf": ["Although ancient Chinese philosophy encompasses diverse schools of thought, its philosophers share several common characteristics."], "text": "Although ancient Chinese philosophy encompasses diverse schools of thought, its philosophers share several common characteristics."}, {"id": "u2_s3_p2", "buf": ["Stress on Human Existence（重人） — Ancient Chinese philosophers studied various philosophical problems grounded in reality and human experience, with many exploring questions related to human existence and the nature of life. For example, Confucianism emphasises the cultivation of virtue and moral disposition as a way to achieve harmony in life and society. Daoism seeks spiritual freedom through alignment with the dao and the practice of naturalness."], "text": "Stress on Human Existence（重人） — Ancient Chinese philosophers studied various philosophical problems grounded in reality and human experience, with many exploring questions related to human existence and the nature of life. For example, Confucianism emphasises the cultivation of virtue and moral disposition as a way to achieve harmony in life and society. Daoism seeks spiritual freedom through alignment with the dao and the practice of naturalness."}, {"id": "u2_s3_p3", "buf": ["Stress on Practice（重行／知行合一） — One of the most important questions that ancient Chinese philosophers explored was the relationship between knowledge and practice. For example, Wang Shouren proposed the concept of the \"unity of knowledge and action (知行合一)\", arguing that knowledge serves as the guiding principle for practice, and practice is the realisation of knowledge in action. Although philosophers differed in their specific understandings of this relationship, they consistently emphasised the importance of integrating knowledge with practice through practical actions.【图注：Wang Shouren（王守仁）】"], "text": "Stress on Practice（重行／知行合一） — One of the most important questions that ancient Chinese philosophers explored was the relationship between knowledge and practice. For example, Wang Shouren proposed the concept of the \"unity of knowledge and action (知行合一)\", arguing that knowledge serves as the guiding principle for practice, and practice is the realisation of knowledge in action. Although philosophers differed in their specific understandings of this relationship, they consistently emphasised the importance of integrating knowledge with practice through practical actions.【图注：Wang Shouren（王守仁）】"}, {"id": "u2_s3_p4", "buf": ["Stress on Morality（重德） — Essentially, every school of ancient Chinese philosophy can be considered a kind of moral philosophy. Almost all of them emphasise moral practice, through which individuals could achieve moral fulfilment and enjoy the benefits of a harmonious social environment such as the monarch's benevolence and the loyalty of officials, the father's kindness and the son's filial piety (孝顺), the elder brother's friendliness and the younger brother's respect, and mutual trust between friends."], "text": "Stress on Morality（重德） — Essentially, every school of ancient Chinese philosophy can be considered a kind of moral philosophy. Almost all of them emphasise moral practice, through which individuals could achieve moral fulfilment and enjoy the benefits of a harmonious social environment such as the monarch's benevolence and the loyalty of officials, the father's kindness and the son's filial piety (孝顺), the elder brother's friendliness and the younger brother's respect, and mutual trust between friends."}, {"id": "u2_s3_p5", "buf": ["Stress on Harmony（重和） — A consistent theme in ancient Chinese philosophy is the emphasis on harmony between man and nature, as well as among people. Confucius highlighted the importance of achieving such harmony through the concept of the Golden Mean. Building on this foundation, Mencius said, \"Opportunities of time granted by Heaven are not equal to advantages of situation afforded by the Earth, and advantages of situation afforded by the Earth are not equal to the union arising from the accord of Men. (天时不如地利，地利不如人和。)\" This clearly expresses the idea that harmony is the highest principle."], "text": "Stress on Harmony（重和） — A consistent theme in ancient Chinese philosophy is the emphasis on harmony between man and nature, as well as among people. Confucius highlighted the importance of achieving such harmony through the concept of the Golden Mean. Building on this foundation, Mencius said, \"Opportunities of time granted by Heaven are not equal to advantages of situation afforded by the Earth, and advantages of situation afforded by the Earth are not equal to the union arising from the accord of Men. (天时不如地利，地利不如人和。)\" This clearly expresses the idea that harmony is the highest principle."}, {"id": "u2_s3_p6", "buf": ["Stress on Intuition（重悟/直觉） — Ancient Chinese philosophy did not adhere to formal structures or systematic frameworks, often expressing ideas in concise and intuitive ways. Philosophical concepts were primarily formed through intuition and deep reflection on life experiences, emphasising practical wisdom over abstract theorising."], "text": "Stress on Intuition（重悟/直觉） — Ancient Chinese philosophy did not adhere to formal structures or systematic frameworks, often expressing ideas in concise and intuitive ways. Philosophical concepts were primarily formed through intuition and deep reflection on life experiences, emphasising practical wisdom over abstract theorising."}, {"id": "u2_s3_p7", "buf": ["Chinese Chan Buddhism (禅宗), which integrates Buddhist teachings with traditional Chinese philosophy, emphasises sudden enlightenment (顿悟) and focuses on self-restraint and mindfulness through practices such as seated meditation (坐禅) and reciting Buddhist scriptures (经文)."], "text": "Chinese Chan Buddhism (禅宗), which integrates Buddhist teachings with traditional Chinese philosophy, emphasises sudden enlightenment (顿悟) and focuses on self-restraint and mindfulness through practices such as seated meditation (坐禅) and reciting Buddhist scriptures (经文)."}]}, {"id": "u2_s4", "title": "中国伦理道德的特征 Characteristics of Chinese Ethics and Morality", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s4_p1", "buf": ["Ethics refers to a set of principles that govern behaviour within a community or society, while morality is an individual's internal principles and values that guide their personal conduct. It is the internal compass that dictates what a person believes is right or wrong, good or bad."], "text": "Ethics refers to a set of principles that govern behaviour within a community or society, while morality is an individual's internal principles and values that guide their personal conduct. It is the internal compass that dictates what a person believes is right or wrong, good or bad."}, {"id": "u2_s4_p2", "buf": ["Traditional Chinese ethics encompasses both enduring wisdom and elements that are no longer suitable for modern society. The Five Constant Virtues (五常; benevolence 仁, righteousness 义, propriety 礼, wisdom 智, and fidelity 信), which advocate benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and fidelity, reflect the high moral standards and ethical wisdom of the Chinese people, offering values of lasting significance. In contrast, the concept of the Three Cardinal Guides (三纲), which emphasises hierarchical relationships in feudal society, is widely regarded as outdated and has been largely abandoned."], "text": "Traditional Chinese ethics encompasses both enduring wisdom and elements that are no longer suitable for modern society. The Five Constant Virtues (五常; benevolence 仁, righteousness 义, propriety 礼, wisdom 智, and fidelity 信), which advocate benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and fidelity, reflect the high moral standards and ethical wisdom of the Chinese people, offering values of lasting significance. In contrast, the concept of the Three Cardinal Guides (三纲), which emphasises hierarchical relationships in feudal society, is widely regarded as outdated and has been largely abandoned."}, {"id": "u2_s4_p3", "buf": ["Seeking Harmony and Maintaining Equilibrium（尚和求平衡） — To the Chinese people, \"harmony\" is a fundamental concept. It embraces diversity, reconciles differences, and fosters balance and unity. At its core, harmony seeks peace and equilibrium in all aspects of life. Maintaining equilibrium — whether in society, relationships, or nature — is the ultimate purpose of harmony."], "text": "Seeking Harmony and Maintaining Equilibrium（尚和求平衡） — To the Chinese people, \"harmony\" is a fundamental concept. It embraces diversity, reconciles differences, and fosters balance and unity. At its core, harmony seeks peace and equilibrium in all aspects of life. Maintaining equilibrium — whether in society, relationships, or nature — is the ultimate purpose of harmony."}, {"id": "u2_s4_p4", "buf": ["Collectivism over Individualism（重集体而轻个人） — Traditional Chinese culture attaches great importance to collective interests, prioritising the needs of society, the nation, and the family above those of the individual or smaller groups. In this cultural framework, an individual's value is often realised through their contributions to the greater whole, as personal identity and purpose are deeply rooted in the collective foundation provided by society."], "text": "Collectivism over Individualism（重集体而轻个人） — Traditional Chinese culture attaches great importance to collective interests, prioritising the needs of society, the nation, and the family above those of the individual or smaller groups. In this cultural framework, an individual's value is often realised through their contributions to the greater whole, as personal identity and purpose are deeply rooted in the collective foundation provided by society."}, {"id": "u2_s4_p5", "buf": ["Spiritual Life over Material Life（重精神而轻物质） — Chinese people deeply value morality in their behaviour. Self-cultivation in moral character is emphasised and considered to be more important than material pursuits. This is reflected in values such as filial piety, kindness towards others, respect for others, and an appreciation for virtuous conduct."], "text": "Spiritual Life over Material Life（重精神而轻物质） — Chinese people deeply value morality in their behaviour. Self-cultivation in moral character is emphasised and considered to be more important than material pursuits. This is reflected in values such as filial piety, kindness towards others, respect for others, and an appreciation for virtuous conduct."}]}]}, {"id": "u3", "title": "Religion 宗教", "sections": [{"id": "u3_s1", "title": "总述", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s1_p1", "buf": ["China is a country with a rich diversity of religions. There are indigenous Daoist beliefs as well as religions of foreign origin, such as Buddhism and Islam. In China, all religions enjoy equal status and coexist harmoniously, with no history of religious conflict. Believers and non-believers alike respect one another and live together in unity and harmony."], "text": "China is a country with a rich diversity of religions. There are indigenous Daoist beliefs as well as religions of foreign origin, such as Buddhism and Islam. In China, all religions enjoy equal status and coexist harmoniously, with no history of religious conflict. Believers and non-believers alike respect one another and live together in unity and harmony."}]}, {"id": "u3_s2", "title": "道教 Daoism", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s2_p1", "buf": ["Daoism originated from a blend of ancient Chinese folk beliefs, philosophical ideas, and the teachings of early thinkers like Laozi and Zhuangzi. Its central tenet is called dao, which represents the ultimate principle and natural order of the universe. One of its foundational scriptures is Daodejing by Laozi."], "text": "Daoism originated from a blend of ancient Chinese folk beliefs, philosophical ideas, and the teachings of early thinkers like Laozi and Zhuangzi. Its central tenet is called dao, which represents the ultimate principle and natural order of the universe. One of its foundational scriptures is Daodejing by Laozi."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p2", "buf": ["It is generally believed that Daoism originated in the late Eastern Han Dynasty. The \"Wu Dou Mi Daoism (五斗米道)\" was founded by Zhang Daoling in Heming Mountain, marking the formal establishment of Daoism, and it became very popular during the Southern and Northern Dynasties. Early followers were required to pay five dou of rice, so it was called the \"Wu Dou Mi Daoism\". Since the believers called Zhang Daoling \"Tianshi (天师)\", the belief became known as \"Tianshi Dao (天师道)\".〔注：dou —— a unit of dry measure equal to 10 litres（斗，容量单位，1斗等于10升）〕"], "text": "It is generally believed that Daoism originated in the late Eastern Han Dynasty. The \"Wu Dou Mi Daoism (五斗米道)\" was founded by Zhang Daoling in Heming Mountain, marking the formal establishment of Daoism, and it became very popular during the Southern and Northern Dynasties. Early followers were required to pay five dou of rice, so it was called the \"Wu Dou Mi Daoism\". Since the believers called Zhang Daoling \"Tianshi (天师)\", the belief became known as \"Tianshi Dao (天师道)\".〔注：dou —— a unit of dry measure equal to 10 litres（斗，容量单位，1斗等于10升）〕"}, {"id": "u3_s2_p3", "buf": ["Daoists revere (尊崇) Laozi as the originator of Daoism and honour him with the title \"Taishang Laojun (太上老君)\"."], "text": "Daoists revere (尊崇) Laozi as the originator of Daoism and honour him with the title \"Taishang Laojun (太上老君)\"."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p4", "buf": ["From the Sui and Tang dynasties to the Northern Song Dynasty, Daoism was particularly under the protection of the royal court. Its social position improved, leading to the widespread establishment of Daoist temples across the country. During the Ming Dynasty, Daoism gradually declined."], "text": "From the Sui and Tang dynasties to the Northern Song Dynasty, Daoism was particularly under the protection of the royal court. Its social position improved, leading to the widespread establishment of Daoist temples across the country. During the Ming Dynasty, Daoism gradually declined."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p5", "buf": ["道观 Daoist Temples", "Daoist temples are places for Daoist priests to carry out ritual activities. The layout of Daoist temples evolved from the traditional square-shaped Chinese courtyard and resembles that of Buddhist temples."], "text": "道观 Daoist Temples\nDaoist temples are places for Daoist priests to carry out ritual activities. The layout of Daoist temples evolved from the traditional square-shaped Chinese courtyard and resembles that of Buddhist temples."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p6", "buf": ["During the early years of Daoism, most Daoist ascetics (修道者) lived in huts and even caves in remote mountains. Today, Daoist buildings can be found on mountain summits (顶) overlooking the sea, in garden- or park-like designs, or in the \"grotto heaven (洞天)\" style. Many scenic mountainous locations and buildings have Daoist names, such as the Immortal Bridge (仙人桥), the Incense Burner Peak (香炉峰), and the Temple of the Eight Immortals (八仙庵)."], "text": "During the early years of Daoism, most Daoist ascetics (修道者) lived in huts and even caves in remote mountains. Today, Daoist buildings can be found on mountain summits (顶) overlooking the sea, in garden- or park-like designs, or in the \"grotto heaven (洞天)\" style. Many scenic mountainous locations and buildings have Daoist names, such as the Immortal Bridge (仙人桥), the Incense Burner Peak (香炉峰), and the Temple of the Eight Immortals (八仙庵)."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p7", "buf": ["〔注〕huabiao（华表）: an ornamental column erected in front of a palace, tomb, etc. In later periods, the huabiao is usually carved with dragon and cloud patterns and topped with a carved horizontal stone slab.（华表，竖立在宫殿、陵墓等前面的装饰性立柱。后世华表通常刻有蟠龙云纹，顶部有横向雕花的石板。）"], "text": "〔注〕huabiao（华表）: an ornamental column erected in front of a palace, tomb, etc. In later periods, the huabiao is usually carved with dragon and cloud patterns and topped with a carved horizontal stone slab.（华表，竖立在宫殿、陵墓等前面的装饰性立柱。后世华表通常刻有蟠龙云纹，顶部有横向雕花的石板。）"}, {"id": "u3_s2_p8", "buf": ["In front of Daoist temples, there are typically mountain gates (山门) and fangan (幡杆) — tall poles used to hang long, narrow flags vertically. In front of large temples granted by imperial decree, huabiao and stone lions were erected, while ordinary small temples were not permitted to build huabiao. Large temples often have side courtyards to the east and west."], "text": "In front of Daoist temples, there are typically mountain gates (山门) and fangan (幡杆) — tall poles used to hang long, narrow flags vertically. In front of large temples granted by imperial decree, huabiao and stone lions were erected, while ordinary small temples were not permitted to build huabiao. Large temples often have side courtyards to the east and west."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p9", "buf": ["The main halls usually stand along the central axis (中轴线), serving as places for people to worship Daoist immortals and perform Daoist rituals and ceremonies. Followers worship the immortals in a manner similar to how Buddhists worship Sakyamuni (释迦牟尼)."], "text": "The main halls usually stand along the central axis (中轴线), serving as places for people to worship Daoist immortals and perform Daoist rituals and ceremonies. Followers worship the immortals in a manner similar to how Buddhists worship Sakyamuni (释迦牟尼)."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p10", "buf": ["八仙 Eight Immortals in Daoism", "The Eight Daoist Immortals are widely worshipped by Daoist believers as well as ordinary people. They are Zhongli Quan (also known as \"Han Zhongli\"), Zhang Guolao, Lu Dongbin, Li Tieguai, He Xiangu, Lan Caihe, Han Xiangzi, and Cao Guojiu. There is a famous saying \"the Eight Immortals crossing the sea, each displaying his or her magical powers\" (八仙过海，各显神通), which demonstrates their divine power."], "text": "八仙 Eight Immortals in Daoism\nThe Eight Daoist Immortals are widely worshipped by Daoist believers as well as ordinary people. They are Zhongli Quan (also known as \"Han Zhongli\"), Zhang Guolao, Lu Dongbin, Li Tieguai, He Xiangu, Lan Caihe, Han Xiangzi, and Cao Guojiu. There is a famous saying \"the Eight Immortals crossing the sea, each displaying his or her magical powers\" (八仙过海，各显神通), which demonstrates their divine power."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p11", "buf": ["For Chinese people, the Eight Immortals symbolise eight social identities: men and women, the old and the young, the rich and the poor, the noble and the humble. This explains why stories about the Eight Immortals have been so popular and influential in Chinese culture."], "text": "For Chinese people, the Eight Immortals symbolise eight social identities: men and women, the old and the young, the rich and the poor, the noble and the humble. This explains why stories about the Eight Immortals have been so popular and influential in Chinese culture."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p12", "buf": ["龙虎山 Dragon Tiger Mountain", "It is believed that Dragon Tiger Mountain is one of the birthplaces of Daoism and is recognised as one of the most sacred mountains in Daoism. According to legend, Zhang Daoling, the founder of Daoism, began distilling elixirs (不老药) here in his pursuit of immortality. When the elixirs were successfully created, a dragon and a tiger appeared above the mountain, giving it its name."], "text": "龙虎山 Dragon Tiger Mountain\nIt is believed that Dragon Tiger Mountain is one of the birthplaces of Daoism and is recognised as one of the most sacred mountains in Daoism. According to legend, Zhang Daoling, the founder of Daoism, began distilling elixirs (不老药) here in his pursuit of immortality. When the elixirs were successfully created, a dragon and a tiger appeared above the mountain, giving it its name."}, {"id": "u3_s2_p13", "buf": ["This mountain is also a key scenic tourist destination, located in the southwestern suburb of Yingtan, Jiangxi Province. Designated as a UNESCO Global Geopark, Dragon Tiger Mountain is a natural site on the World Heritage List."], "text": "This mountain is also a key scenic tourist destination, located in the southwestern suburb of Yingtan, Jiangxi Province. Designated as a UNESCO Global Geopark, Dragon Tiger Mountain is a natural site on the World Heritage List."}]}, {"id": "u3_s3", "title": "佛教 Buddhism", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s3_p1", "buf": ["Buddhism originated in ancient India in the fifth century BCE. Sakyamuni is said to be the founder of Buddhism. As the story goes, he was a prince who, when he was young, witnessed the suffering of many due to poverty, pain, sickness, and death. Around the age of 29, he chose to abandon the material world in search of enlightenment. Sakyamuni sat under a bodhi tree (菩提树) and fell into deep meditation (冥想). Eventually, he achieved enlightenment and became a Buddha."], "text": "Buddhism originated in ancient India in the fifth century BCE. Sakyamuni is said to be the founder of Buddhism. As the story goes, he was a prince who, when he was young, witnessed the suffering of many due to poverty, pain, sickness, and death. Around the age of 29, he chose to abandon the material world in search of enlightenment. Sakyamuni sat under a bodhi tree (菩提树) and fell into deep meditation (冥想). Eventually, he achieved enlightenment and became a Buddha."}, {"id": "u3_s3_p2", "buf": ["It is commonly believed that Buddhism was first introduced into China around the first century. During the Sui and Tang dynasties, Buddhism entered its golden age, with an abundance of Buddhist temples built nationwide. As Buddhism was declining in India at that time, China became a centre of the Buddhist world. Buddhism has long coexisted with Confucianism and Daoism in China."], "text": "It is commonly believed that Buddhism was first introduced into China around the first century. During the Sui and Tang dynasties, Buddhism entered its golden age, with an abundance of Buddhist temples built nationwide. As Buddhism was declining in India at that time, China became a centre of the Buddhist world. Buddhism has long coexisted with Confucianism and Daoism in China."}, {"id": "u3_s3_p3", "buf": ["佛教寺院 Buddhist Temples", "Chinese Buddhist architecture includes pagodas (塔), temples, and grottoes (石窟). Early Buddhist temples often integrated pagodas into courtyard-style layouts. As the style evolved, later temples began to resemble traditional Chinese residences and palaces. These temples adopted a layout centred around courtyards, which made them different from temples in India or other Buddhist countries. Typically, Buddhist temples are built along an invisible central axis and face south. The front hall usually houses four statues, known as the Four Heavenly Kings (四大天王), while the statue of the Maitreya Buddha (弥勒佛, also known as the Laughing Buddha) is located at the centre of the hall."], "text": "佛教寺院 Buddhist Temples\nChinese Buddhist architecture includes pagodas (塔), temples, and grottoes (石窟). Early Buddhist temples often integrated pagodas into courtyard-style layouts. As the style evolved, later temples began to resemble traditional Chinese residences and palaces. These temples adopted a layout centred around courtyards, which made them different from temples in India or other Buddhist countries. Typically, Buddhist temples are built along an invisible central axis and face south. The front hall usually houses four statues, known as the Four Heavenly Kings (四大天王), while the statue of the Maitreya Buddha (弥勒佛, also known as the Laughing Buddha) is located at the centre of the hall."}, {"id": "u3_s3_p4", "buf": ["〔注〕muyu（木鱼）: a fish-shaped percussion instrument made from a hollow wooden block, used by Buddhist monks to beat rhythm while they chant scriptures.（木鱼，一种打击乐器，由一个空心木块制成，佛教僧侣在念经时用来打节拍。）"], "text": "〔注〕muyu（木鱼）: a fish-shaped percussion instrument made from a hollow wooden block, used by Buddhist monks to beat rhythm while they chant scriptures.（木鱼，一种打击乐器，由一个空心木块制成，佛教僧侣在念经时用来打节拍。）"}, {"id": "u3_s3_p5", "buf": ["The Mahavira Hall (大雄宝殿) is dedicated to the statue of Sakyamuni and other Buddhas. It features a main altar for daily Buddhist ceremonies and text recitals (诵经). Rows of cotton cushions (蒲团) are placed below the altar, accompanied by a bell, a drum, and a muyu. The Eighteen Arhats (十八罗汉) stand against the east and west walls of the hall. It is said that they each have individual supernatural powers."], "text": "The Mahavira Hall (大雄宝殿) is dedicated to the statue of Sakyamuni and other Buddhas. It features a main altar for daily Buddhist ceremonies and text recitals (诵经). Rows of cotton cushions (蒲团) are placed below the altar, accompanied by a bell, a drum, and a muyu. The Eighteen Arhats (十八罗汉) stand against the east and west walls of the hall. It is said that they each have individual supernatural powers."}, {"id": "u3_s3_p6", "buf": ["The White Horse Temple, located to the east of Luoyang, Henan Province, was the first officially built Buddhist temple in China during the Eastern Han Dynasty. According to historical accounts, Emperor Ming of the Eastern Han Dynasty sent envoys (使者) to ancient India to learn about Buddhism. The envoys returned to Luoyang, which was then the capital of China, with Buddhist scriptures and statues carried by a white horse. The temple was named after this white horse."], "text": "The White Horse Temple, located to the east of Luoyang, Henan Province, was the first officially built Buddhist temple in China during the Eastern Han Dynasty. According to historical accounts, Emperor Ming of the Eastern Han Dynasty sent envoys (使者) to ancient India to learn about Buddhism. The envoys returned to Luoyang, which was then the capital of China, with Buddhist scriptures and statues carried by a white horse. The temple was named after this white horse."}, {"id": "u3_s3_p7", "buf": ["佛教名山 Buddhist Mountains", "Among the many Buddhist mountains in China, Mount Wutai, Mount Emei, Mount Putuo, and Mount Jiuhua are recognised as the Four Sacred Buddhist Mountains. Each year, large numbers of visitors from all over the world come to these mountains for sightseeing or to gain a deeper understanding of Chinese culture."], "text": "佛教名山 Buddhist Mountains\nAmong the many Buddhist mountains in China, Mount Wutai, Mount Emei, Mount Putuo, and Mount Jiuhua are recognised as the Four Sacred Buddhist Mountains. Each year, large numbers of visitors from all over the world come to these mountains for sightseeing or to gain a deeper understanding of Chinese culture."}]}, {"id": "u3_s4", "title": "伊斯兰教 Islam", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s4_p1", "buf": ["Islam was founded by the Arab prophet Muhammad and was introduced into China in the seventh century. For Muslims, there is only one God, Allah (安拉). Muslims accept and submit to the will of Allah, believing that Allah has communicated with humanity through chosen messengers throughout history. However, it was Muhammad who delivered the divine message and established a social order."], "text": "Islam was founded by the Arab prophet Muhammad and was introduced into China in the seventh century. For Muslims, there is only one God, Allah (安拉). Muslims accept and submit to the will of Allah, believing that Allah has communicated with humanity through chosen messengers throughout history. However, it was Muhammad who delivered the divine message and established a social order."}, {"id": "u3_s4_p2", "buf": ["清真寺 Mosques", "A mosque (清真寺) is typically a high-domed building with sweeping arches, flowing arabesques (阿拉伯式花饰), and towering minarets (宣礼塔)."], "text": "清真寺 Mosques\nA mosque (清真寺) is typically a high-domed building with sweeping arches, flowing arabesques (阿拉伯式花饰), and towering minarets (宣礼塔)."}, {"id": "u3_s4_p3", "buf": ["Although there are variations, the major architectural components of mosques are generally consistent. Inside, a mihrab or prayer niche (壁龛), symbolising the place where the prophet led prayers, is set into the middle of the qibla wall (朝拜墙) to indicate the direction of Mecca. Outside, there is usually a minaret from which the faithful are called to pray, along with a place for the ablutions (净礼) required before prayer, typically equipped with running water."], "text": "Although there are variations, the major architectural components of mosques are generally consistent. Inside, a mihrab or prayer niche (壁龛), symbolising the place where the prophet led prayers, is set into the middle of the qibla wall (朝拜墙) to indicate the direction of Mecca. Outside, there is usually a minaret from which the faithful are called to pray, along with a place for the ablutions (净礼) required before prayer, typically equipped with running water."}, {"id": "u3_s4_p4", "buf": ["传入中国", "Islam was brought to China peacefully. Arab traders who landed on the southern coast of China established mosques in major maritime cities like Guangzhou and Quanzhou, while Muslim merchants travelling along the Silk Road through Central Asia converted (使皈依) many Chinese in the north. The Great Mosque in Xi'an is one of the oldest, largest, and best-preserved Islamic mosques in China."], "text": "传入中国\nIslam was brought to China peacefully. Arab traders who landed on the southern coast of China established mosques in major maritime cities like Guangzhou and Quanzhou, while Muslim merchants travelling along the Silk Road through Central Asia converted (使皈依) many Chinese in the north. The Great Mosque in Xi'an is one of the oldest, largest, and best-preserved Islamic mosques in China."}, {"id": "u3_s4_p5", "buf": ["Most of China's Muslims live in Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region, Gansu Province, Qinghai Province, Henan Province, and Yunnan Province.【图注：Dongguan Grand Mosque in Xining（西宁东关清真大寺）】"], "text": "Most of China's Muslims live in Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region, Gansu Province, Qinghai Province, Henan Province, and Yunnan Province.【图注：Dongguan Grand Mosque in Xining（西宁东关清真大寺）】"}]}]}], "chunks": [], "vocabA": [{"pos": "形", "diff": "全员", "term": "indigenous", "zh": "本土的", "q": "Culturally, indigenous traditions, such as Confucianism and Daoism, have profoundly influenced the philosophy, literature, and arts of the Chinese people.", "tr": "从文化上看，儒家、道家等本土传统，连同佛教等外来宗教，数百年来深刻影响了中国人民的哲学、文学与艺术。", "src": "章节衔接导语"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "philosophy", "zh": "哲学", "q": "Chinese philosophy is one of the three major philosophical traditions in the world.", "tr": "中国哲学是世界上三大哲学传统之一，另外两大分别是西方哲学和印度哲学。", "src": "引言与百家争鸣"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "school of thought", "zh": "学派", "q": "Chinese philosophy is the collective designation for various schools of thought.", "tr": "中国哲学是对各种思想学派的总体称谓。", "src": "引言与百家争鸣"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "the contention of a hundred schools of thought", "zh": "百家争鸣", "q": "Chinese philosophy flourished with the emergence of \"the contention of a hundred schools of thought\".", "tr": "中国哲学随着\"百家争鸣\"局面的出现而兴盛起来。", "src": "引言与百家争鸣"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "emerge", "zh": "出现；相继兴起", "q": "During this time, various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Legalism, Daoism, and Mohism, emerged.", "tr": "这一时期，儒家、法家、道家和墨家等学派相继出现。", "src": "引言与百家争鸣"}, {"pos": "副", "diff": "中", "term": "vigorously", "zh": "积极地；充满活力地", "q": "Scholars wrote, lectured, and debated vigorously, creating a lively academic environment.", "tr": "学者们积极著书立说、讲学辩论，营造出一种活跃的学术氛围。", "src": "引言与百家争鸣"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "characterise", "zh": "以……为特征", "q": "Philosophy in the pre-Qin period was characterised by the emergence of various ancient philosophical schools of thought.", "tr": "先秦时期的哲学以各种古代哲学学派的涌现为特征。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "Confucianism", "zh": "儒家", "q": "Confucianism is a school of thought represented by Confucius and Mencius.", "tr": "儒家是由孔子和孟子所代表的学派。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "benevolence", "zh": "仁", "q": "The school advocates core values such as benevolence, righteousness, loyalty, empathy, and the Golden Mean.", "tr": "该学派倡导仁、义、忠、恕以及中庸之道等核心价值观。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "the Golden Mean", "zh": "中庸之道", "q": "According to Confucius, the \"mean\" does not imply \"compromise\" but rather a \"moderate\" and \"just right\" approach.", "tr": "在孔子看来，\"中庸\"并非\"妥协\"，而是\"恰到好处\"地理解和处理客观事物的方式。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "embrace", "zh": "信奉；接受", "q": "Neighbouring countries such as Japan and Vietnam also embrace Confucianism in their national life and culture.", "tr": "日本、越南等邻国也在其国民生活和文化中信奉儒家学说。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "全员", "term": "Daoism", "zh": "道家；道教", "q": "Daoism is a school of thought that draws from the teachings of Laozi and Zhuangzi.", "tr": "道家是源于老子和庄子学说的学派。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "conform (to)", "zh": "遵从；遵循", "q": "All people should conform to the dao, which is the course and standard of all things.", "tr": "万事万物都应遵循\"道\"，道是万物的法则与标准。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "overindulgence", "zh": "过度沉溺", "q": "Daoists believe that overindulgence in these pursuits can bring worries and troubles.", "tr": "道家认为，过度沉溺于这些追求会带来忧虑和烦恼。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "non-action", "zh": "无为", "q": "Daoism promotes the political principle of \"achieving good governance through non-action\".", "tr": "道家提倡\"无为而治\"的政治理念。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "Mohism", "zh": "墨家", "q": "Mohism, based on the teachings of Mozi, emphasises universal love.", "tr": "墨家以墨子学说为基础，强调兼爱。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "universal love", "zh": "兼爱", "q": "This principle encourages people to love others, including their families and people of other states, as they love themselves.", "tr": "这一原则鼓励人们像爱自己一样去爱他人，包括自己的家人和别国的人民。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中强", "term": "reject", "zh": "反对；摒弃", "q": "In politics and ethics, Mohism advocates honouring virtuous people, rejecting fatalism and aggressive wars.", "tr": "在政治与伦理上，墨家主张尊贤，反对宿命论和侵略战争。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "Legalism", "zh": "法家", "q": "Legalism, with Hanfeizi as one of its important representatives, believes that it is necessary to use strict laws to keep the state in order.", "tr": "法家以韩非子为重要代表，认为必须用严明的法律来维持国家秩序。", "src": "先秦子学"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中强", "term": "reject the other schools of thought and respect only Confucianism", "zh": "罢黜百家，独尊儒术", "q": "In the Western Han Dynasty, Emperor Wudi adopted Dong Zhongshu's proposal of rejecting the other schools of thought and respecting only Confucianism.", "tr": "西汉时期，汉武帝采纳了董仲舒\"罢黜百家，独尊儒术\"的主张。", "src": "两汉经学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "ideology", "zh": "意识形态；官方思想", "q": "Confucianism rose from among many schools of thought to become the official ideology.", "tr": "儒家从诸多学派中脱颖而出，成为官方意识形态。", "src": "两汉经学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "metaphysics", "zh": "玄学；形而上学", "q": "Metaphysics in China mainly integrates the thoughts of Confucianism and Daoism.", "tr": "中国的玄学主要融合了儒家和道家的思想。", "src": "魏晋玄学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "noumenon", "zh": "本体", "q": "To explore the noumenon of the universe was the theme of metaphysics in this period.", "tr": "探索宇宙本体是这一时期玄学的主题。", "src": "魏晋玄学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "apex", "zh": "顶点；顶峰", "q": "During the Sui and Tang dynasties, Buddhism reached its apex in China.", "tr": "隋唐时期，佛教在中国达到了鼎盛。", "src": "隋唐佛学"}, {"pos": "形", "diff": "中强", "term": "Neo-Confucianism", "zh": "新儒学（宋明理学）", "q": "Neo-Confucianism integrated elements of Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism.", "tr": "宋明理学融合了儒、道、佛三家的思想。", "src": "宋明理学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "rationalism", "zh": "天理（理学）", "q": "The School of Principle argued that \"rationalism\" is eternal and exists as a spiritual essence prior to the material world.", "tr": "理学派认为\"天理\"是永恒的，是先于物质世界而存在的精神本源。", "src": "宋明理学"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "subjective consciousness", "zh": "主观意识", "q": "They argued that subjective consciousness was the origin of all things in the world.", "tr": "他们主张主观意识是世界万物的本源。", "src": "宋明理学"}, {"pos": "形", "diff": "中", "term": "practicality", "zh": "实用；经世致用", "q": "The school of application philosophy emphasised the promotion of practicality and the rejection of abstraction.", "tr": "实学派强调倡导实用、反对抽象空谈。", "src": "明清实学"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "cultivate", "zh": "培养；修养", "q": "Confucianism emphasises the cultivation of virtue and moral disposition.", "tr": "儒家强调修养品德与道德操守。", "src": "基本特征·重人"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "the unity of knowledge and action", "zh": "知行合一", "q": "Wang Shouren proposed the concept of the \"unity of knowledge and action\".", "tr": "王守仁提出了\"知行合一\"的思想。", "src": "基本特征·重行"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "filial piety", "zh": "孝顺", "q": "The father's kindness and the son's filial piety reflect the harmony of family life.", "tr": "父慈子孝体现了家庭生活的和谐。", "src": "基本特征·重德"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "harmony", "zh": "和谐；和", "q": "A consistent theme in ancient Chinese philosophy is the emphasis on harmony between man and nature.", "tr": "中国古代哲学一以贯之的主题，是强调人与自然的和谐。", "src": "基本特征·重和"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "intuition", "zh": "直觉；顿悟", "q": "Philosophical concepts were primarily formed through intuition and deep reflection on life experiences.", "tr": "哲学概念主要通过直觉以及对生活经验的深刻反思而形成。", "src": "基本特征·重悟"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "seated meditation", "zh": "坐禅", "q": "Chinese Chan Buddhism emphasises sudden enlightenment and focuses on self-restraint and mindfulness through seated meditation.", "tr": "中国禅宗强调顿悟，主张通过坐禅等修行方式来实现自我克制与静心。", "src": "基本特征·重悟"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "ethics", "zh": "伦理", "q": "Ethics refers to a set of principles that govern behaviour within a community or society.", "tr": "伦理是指在一个社群或社会中规范行为的规则体系。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "morality", "zh": "道德", "q": "Morality is an individual's internal principles and values that guide their personal conduct.", "tr": "道德是指导个人行为的内在原则与价值观。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "the Five Constant Virtues", "zh": "五常", "q": "The Five Constant Virtues advocate benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and fidelity.", "tr": "五常倡导仁、义、礼、智、信。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "collectivism", "zh": "集体主义", "q": "Traditional Chinese culture attaches great importance to collective interests.", "tr": "中国传统文化高度重视集体利益。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "prioritise", "zh": "以…为先；优先考虑", "q": "Traditional Chinese culture prioritises the needs of society, the nation, and the family above those of the individual.", "tr": "中国传统文化把社会、国家和家庭的需要置于个人需要之上。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "self-cultivation", "zh": "修身；自我修养", "q": "Self-cultivation in moral character is emphasised and considered to be more important than material pursuits.", "tr": "人们重视道德品性的修养，认为它比物质追求更为重要。", "src": "伦理与道德的特征"}], "vocabB": [{"pos": "形", "diff": "中", "term": "diverse", "zh": "多样的", "q": "China is a country with a rich diversity of religions.", "tr": "中国是一个宗教非常多元化的国家。", "src": "宗教总述"}, {"pos": "副", "diff": "中", "term": "harmoniously", "zh": "和谐地", "q": "In China, all religions enjoy equal status and coexist harmoniously.", "tr": "在中国，各宗教享有平等地位，和谐共存。", "src": "宗教总述"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "tenet", "zh": "信条；教义", "q": "Its central tenet is called dao, which represents the ultimate principle and natural order of the universe.", "tr": "其核心教义是\"道\"，\"道\"代表着宇宙的终极法则和自然秩序。", "src": "道教"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "scripture", "zh": "经书；经文", "q": "One of its foundational scriptures is Daodejing by Laozi.", "tr": "其一部根本经典是老子的《道德经》。", "src": "道教"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "found", "zh": "创立；创建", "q": "\"Wu Dou Mi Daoism\" was founded by Zhang Daoling in Heming Mountain.", "tr": "张道陵在鹤鸣山创立了\"五斗米道\"。", "src": "道教"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "revere", "zh": "尊崇；崇敬", "q": "Daoists revere Laozi as the originator of Daoism.", "tr": "道士尊崇老子为道教的创始人。", "src": "道教"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "ascetic", "zh": "修道者", "q": "During the early years of Daoism, most Daoist ascetics lived in huts and even caves in remote mountains.", "tr": "道教创立初期，大多数修道者住在茅屋甚至深山洞穴之中。", "src": "道观"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "summit", "zh": "山顶；峰顶", "q": "Daoist temples can be found on mountain summits overlooking the sea.", "tr": "道观可以建在高耸入云、俯视大海的山巅之上。", "src": "道观"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "immortal", "zh": "神仙；仙人", "q": "The main halls serve as places for people to worship Daoist immortals and perform Daoist rituals.", "tr": "主殿是信徒供奉道教神仙、举行道教法事的地方。", "src": "道观"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "elixir", "zh": "不老药；仙丹", "q": "Zhang Daoling began distilling elixirs here in his pursuit of immortality.", "tr": "张道陵在此处炼制不老药，以求长生不老。", "src": "龙虎山"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "enlightenment", "zh": "觉悟；正觉", "q": "Around the age of 29, he chose to abandon the material world in search of enlightenment.", "tr": "大约二十九岁时，他选择舍弃尘世，寻求觉悟。", "src": "佛教"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "meditation", "zh": "冥想；禅定", "q": "Sakyamuni sat under a bodhi tree and fell into deep meditation.", "tr": "释迦牟尼坐在菩提树下，进入深度禅定。", "src": "佛教"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "pagoda", "zh": "宝塔", "q": "Chinese Buddhist architecture includes pagodas, temples, and grottoes.", "tr": "中国的佛教建筑包括宝塔、寺院和石窟。", "src": "佛教寺院"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "grotto", "zh": "石窟", "q": "Early Buddhist temples often integrated pagodas into courtyard-style layouts.", "tr": "早期的佛教寺院常将宝塔融入庭院式的布局之中。", "src": "佛教寺院"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "altar", "zh": "佛坛；祭坛", "q": "The Mahavira Hall features a main altar for daily Buddhist ceremonies.", "tr": "大雄宝殿设有主佛坛，用于日常佛教法事。", "src": "佛教寺院"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "envoy", "zh": "使者", "q": "Emperor Ming of the Eastern Han Dynasty sent envoys to ancient India to learn about Buddhism.", "tr": "东汉明帝派遣使者前往古印度学习佛法。", "src": "佛教寺院"}, {"pos": "形", "diff": "中", "term": "sacred", "zh": "神圣的", "q": "Mount Wutai, Mount Emei, Mount Putuo, and Mount Jiuhua are recognised as the Four Sacred Buddhist Mountains.", "tr": "五台山、峨眉山、普陀山和九华山被公认为佛教四大名山。", "src": "佛教名山"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "prophet", "zh": "先知", "q": "Islam was founded by the Arab prophet Muhammad.", "tr": "伊斯兰教由阿拉伯先知穆罕默德创立。", "src": "伊斯兰教"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中", "term": "submit (to)", "zh": "顺从；臣服", "q": "Muslims accept and submit to the will of Allah.", "tr": "穆斯林接受并顺从安拉的意志。", "src": "伊斯兰教"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中强", "term": "mosque", "zh": "清真寺", "q": "A mosque is typically a high-domed building with sweeping arches and towering minarets.", "tr": "清真寺通常是高耸圆顶的建筑，带有拱券和高耸的宣礼塔。", "src": "清真寺"}, {"pos": "名", "diff": "中", "term": "minaret", "zh": "宣礼塔", "q": "Outside, there is usually a minaret from which the faithful are called to pray.", "tr": "寺外通常建有宣礼塔，塔上召唤信众前来礼拜。", "src": "清真寺"}, {"pos": "动", "diff": "中强", "term": "convert", "zh": "使皈依", "q": "Muslim merchants travelling along the Silk Road converted many Chinese in the north.", "tr": "沿丝绸之路往来的穆斯林商贾使北方许多中国人皈依了伊斯兰教。", "src": "传入中国"}], "examsA": [{"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国有句老话：“一日为师，终身为父”。", "en": "There is an old saying in China: \"A teacher for a day is a father for a lifetime.\"", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→There is);文化:一日为师，终身为父→S4 a teacher for a day is a father for a lifetime(谚语，库内仅作参考通译)；备注:[原卷如此]（引号为全角“”，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语（There be 句，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（冒号引出引语）。", "te": "增补（There is / an old saying in China）；惯用等值（\"老话\"→an old saying）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"一日为师，终身为父\"为谚语，无官方英文原句，标 S4（a teacher for a day is a father for a lifetime，库内参考通译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国人的观念里，老师不仅传授知识，更教给学生做人的道理。", "en": "In the view of the Chinese people, teachers not only impart knowledge but also teach students the morals of life.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"老师传授知识\"/②\"更教给学生做人的道理\";核心=并列;触发[不仅…更]→not only … but also …;话题:句首框架状语(在中国人的观念里)前置;补主语(→teachers);文化:做人的道理→S1 morals(CGTN \"instilling knowledge and morals\")", "ta": "状语前置；补主语；触发构式（not only…but also…，认知构式翻译观）。", "te": "增补（the morals of life）；动词\"传授\"→impart。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"做人的道理\"对应官方短语见 CGTN（morals），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在古代，学生初次见老师时要举行隆重的仪式，足见老师地位之高。", "en": "In ancient times, students held a grand ceremony when they first met their teachers, which shows the high status of teachers.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"学生…举行隆重的仪式\"/②\"足见老师地位之高\";核心=①;②→非限定定从(which shows…)|分词;逻辑:结果/评注→which shows;话题:句首框架状语(在古代)前置;补主语(→students);时锚:古代→一般过去时", "ta": "状语前置；补主语；意合→形合（非限定 which，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（一般过去时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "词性转换（\"老师地位之高\"→the high status of teachers）；增补（grand）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"教育/师道\"见库内 S1/S3。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "为弘扬尊师的优良传统，中国从1985年开始将每年的9月10日定为教师节。", "en": "To carry forward the fine tradition of respecting teachers, China designated September 10 as Teachers' Day starting from 1985.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"为弘扬尊师的优良传统\"/②\"中国…将…定为教师节\";核心=②;①→目的状语(To V…)(CL-07);话题:补主语(→China);时锚:1985年开始→一般过去时|现在完成时;文化:尊师→S1 respecting and honoring teachers(CGTN)；优良传统→fine tradition(S1 China Daily)；教师节→S1 Teachers' Day(定译)", "ta": "目的状语前置（To V，连淑能 1993）；补主语；时态呼应。", "te": "增补（starting from）；动词\"定为\"→designated。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"尊师\"见 CGTN（respecting and honoring teachers），S1；\"弘扬…优良传统\"见 China Daily Asia（carry forward the fine tradition of…），S1；\"教师节\"官方定译 Teachers' Day，S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在这一天，学生以各种方式表达对老师的敬意。", "en": "On this day, students express their respect for teachers in various ways.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:句首框架状语(在这一天)前置;补主语(→students);逻辑:方式状语→in various ways", "ta": "状语前置；补主语。", "te": "增补（their）；词性转换（\"敬意\"→respect）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "尊师是中华民族的传统美德，在当今社会得到了发扬光大。", "en": "Respecting teachers is a traditional virtue of the Chinese nation and has been further promoted in today's society.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "尊师", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and)/被动补语;话题:动名词主语(→Respecting teachers)(CL-02);受事→被动(得到发扬光大→has been promoted);文化:中华民族→S1 the Chinese nation；传统美德→traditional virtues(S3);时锚:当今→现在完成时", "ta": "动名词主语；受事→被动（has been promoted，CL-04）；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时）。", "te": "增补（further）；动词\"发扬光大\"→promoted。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"中华民族\"见国新办白皮书，S1；\"传统美德\"见库内 S3。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "餐桌礼仪 (dining etiquette) 是中华传统文化的重要组成部分，蕴含着热情有礼的待客之道。", "en": "Dining etiquette is an important part of traditional Chinese culture, embodying a warm and courteous hospitality.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "餐桌礼仪", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"餐桌礼仪是…组成部分\"/②\"蕴含着…待客之道\";核心=①;②→分词状语(embodying…)(CL-03)|定语;话题:补主语(→Dining etiquette);文化:餐桌礼仪→dining etiquette(原卷括注，同源优先)；待客之道→S1 hospitality(China Daily)；传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（embodying…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "增补（important / warm）；词性转换（\"重要组成部分\"→an important part of）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"餐桌礼仪\"从原卷括注 dining etiquette（同源优先）；\"待客之道\"见 China Daily（hospitality），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "为了招待客人，主人会根据客人的口味和喜好准备丰富精致的菜肴。", "en": "To entertain guests, the host prepares rich and exquisite dishes according to their tastes and preferences.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "餐桌礼仪", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"为了招待客人\"/②\"主人…准备…菜肴\";核心=②;①→目的状语(To V…)(CL-07);话题:补主语(→the host);文化:菜肴→dishes", "ta": "目的状语前置（To V，连淑能 1993）；补主语。", "te": "增补（rich and exquisite）；词性转换（\"客人的\"→their）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "就餐座位的安排非常讲究，主人坐在正对大门的位置，在主人身边就座的是最重要的客人。", "en": "The seating arrangement at a meal is very particular: the host sits facing the door, and the most important guest sits beside the host.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "餐桌礼仪", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→说明/举例(冒号＋and);话题:补主语(→The seating arrangement)；文化:座次安排→S1 seating arrangements(China Daily)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（冒号＋and，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（at a meal）；词性转换（\"非常讲究\"→is very particular）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"座次安排\"对应官方短语见 China Daily（seating arrangements），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "进餐时，主人会不时地为客人添酒加菜。", "en": "During the meal, the host will refill the guests' glasses and add dishes from time to time.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "餐桌礼仪", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:句首框架状语(进餐时)前置;补主语(→the host);文化:添酒加菜→S4 refill the guests' glasses and add dishes(库内按语境编译)", "ta": "状语前置；补主语。", "te": "增补（from time to time）；惯用等值（\"添酒加菜\"→refill … glasses and add dishes）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"添酒加菜\"无官方对应固定表达，标 S4（refill the guests' glasses and add dishes）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这种独特的餐桌礼仪已延续了数千年，对促进和谐的人际交往起到了重要作用。", "en": "This unique dining etiquette has continued for thousands of years, playing an important role in promoting harmonious interpersonal interactions.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "餐桌礼仪", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→分词状语(playing an important role in…)(CL-03);话题:补主语(→This unique dining etiquette);时锚:已延续数千年→现在完成时;文化:人际交往→interpersonal interactions", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（playing…，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（unique）；词性转换（\"促进\"→promoting）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国传统文化中，亲朋好友之间互赠礼物是人际交往的一种重要礼节 (etiquette)。", "en": "In traditional Chinese culture, exchanging gifts between relatives and friends is an important etiquette in interpersonal interactions.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "互赠礼物", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:句首框架状语(在中国传统文化中)前置;动名词主语(→Exchanging gifts…)(CL-02);文化:互赠礼物→S1 exchange gifts(China Daily)；礼节→etiquette(原卷括注，同源优先)；传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture", "ta": "状语前置；动名词主语（Exchanging gifts…，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（between relatives and friends）；词性转换（\"人际交往\"→interpersonal interactions）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"互赠礼物\"见 China Daily（exchange gifts），S1；\"礼节\"从原卷括注 etiquette（同源优先）；\"中国传统文化\"见 China Daily，S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "赠送礼物不仅是物质的馈赠，更是情感的表达，是维系人际关系的重要纽带。", "en": "Giving gifts is not only a material present but also an expression of emotion and an important bond that maintains interpersonal relationships.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "互赠礼物", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②→递进(but also…);③→并列/同位(and a bond…);触发[不仅…更是]→not only … but also …;话题:动名词主语(→Giving gifts)(CL-02);逻辑:递进→not only…but also…", "ta": "动名词主语；触发构式（not only…but also…，认知构式翻译观）；意合→形合；修饰语后置（that maintains…）。", "te": "增补（material）；词性转换（\"馈赠\"→a present）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "一份精心挑选、符合接受者喜好的礼物，往往能加深彼此的感情。", "en": "A well-chosen gift that suits the recipient's tastes often deepens the bond between the two parties.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "互赠礼物", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:长定语前置→主语(A gift that…)|修饰后置;补主语(→A gift);文化:接受者→recipient", "ta": "修饰语后置（that suits…，连淑能 1993）；补主语。", "te": "增补（well-chosen / often）；词性转换（\"感情/关系\"→the bond）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国人遵循“礼尚往来” (mutually exchanging courtesies) 的传统，接受礼物时会表达谢意，并适时回赠礼物，以增进彼此的融洽关系。", "en": "The Chinese follow the tradition of \"reciprocity,\" expressing thanks when receiving a gift and giving one in return at a proper time to enhance their harmonious relationship.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "互赠礼物", "set": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2026·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②③④;核心=①;②③→分词状语(expressing…)＋并列;④→目的状语(to V…)(CL-07);话题:补主语(→The Chinese);逻辑:目的→to V;文化:礼尚往来→S3 reciprocity(库内首选)|原卷括注 mutually exchanging courtesies；融洽关系→harmonious relationship；备注:[原卷如此]（引号为全角“”，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（expressing…）/目的状语（to enhance…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "增补（in return / at a proper time）；惯用等值（\"适时\"→at a proper time）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"礼尚往来\"库内首选 S3 reciprocity，原卷另括注 mutually exchanging courtesies。"}], "examsB": [{"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "尊老是中华民族的传统美德，深深植根于中国人的思想和行为中，是人们普遍遵守的行为规范和社会准则。", "en": "Respecting the elderly is a traditional virtue of the Chinese nation, deeply rooted in the thoughts and behaviour of the Chinese people and a widely observed code of conduct and social norm.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"尊老是…传统美德\"/②\"深深植根于…思想和行为中\"/③\"是…行为规范和社会准则\";核心=①;②→分词状语(deeply rooted in…)(CL-03);③→并列非限定(and a … code of conduct)|定语从句;话题:动名词主语(→Respecting the elderly)(CL-02);文化:尊老→S1 respect the elderly(CGTN 家风篇)；中华民族→S1 the Chinese nation；传统美德→traditional virtues(S3)", "ta": "动名词主语（\"尊老\"→Respecting the elderly，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（逗号并句→分词/并列，连淑能 1993）；修饰语后置。", "te": "增补（the Chinese nation / widely，Amplification，Molina & Albir 2002）；词性转换（\"行为规范\"→a code of conduct）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"尊老\"对应官方短语见 CGTN（cgtn.com），S1；\"中华民族\"见国新办白皮书（scio.gov.cn），S1；\"传统美德\"见库内 S3。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在当今的中国，这种美德得到广泛传承。", "en": "In today's China, this virtue is widely carried forward.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:当今→一般现在时;话题:句首框架状语(在…中国)前置;受事→被动(得到传承→is carried forward);文化:传承→carry forward(S1 新华/中国网)", "ta": "受事→被动（is carried forward，CL-04）；状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（widely）；动词\"传承\"→carry forward。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"传承/传承弘扬\"对应官方短语见新华/中国网（inherit and carry forward），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "社会各界积极营造敬老助老的社会氛围。", "en": "All sectors of society are actively fostering a social atmosphere of respecting and helping the elderly.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→All sectors of society);文化:敬老助老→S4 respecting and helping the elderly(通译)；社会氛围→S4 social atmosphere", "ta": "补主语（All sectors of society，Li & Thompson 1976）；修饰语后置（of respecting…）。", "te": "增补（actively）；词性转换（\"营造氛围\"→foster an atmosphere）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"敬老助老\"无 whitelist 定译，标 S4（respecting and helping the elderly 通译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "为老年人提供便利服务已成为社会共识，例如社区专门开设长者食堂，公共场所配置优先座位。", "en": "Providing convenient services for the elderly has become a social consensus; for example, communities have opened special canteens for the elderly, and public places provide priority seats.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"为老年人提供便利服务已成为社会共识\"/②\"社区专门开设长者食堂\"/③\"公共场所配置优先座位\";核心=①;②③→举例(for example＋并列);话题:动名词主语(→Providing convenient services for the elderly)(CL-02);文化:长者食堂→community canteens(S1 库内「社区食堂」gov 英文版，语境适配)；优先座位→S4 priority seats", "ta": "动名词主语（Providing…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（分号＋for example＋and）；补主语。", "te": "增补（special / for example）；词性转换（\"开设\"→have opened）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"社区食堂/长者食堂\"对应官方短语见 gov 英文版（community canteens），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "政府还出台了一系列政策，为老年人的权益提供有力保障。", "en": "The government has also rolled out a series of policies to provide strong protection for the rights and interests of the elderly.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"政府…出台了一系列政策\"/②\"为老年人的权益提供有力保障\";核心=①;②→目的状语(to V…)(CL-07)|伴随;逻辑:目的→to V;话题:补主语(→The government);时锚:还/已出台→现在完成时;文化:权益→rights and interests", "ta": "补主语；目的状语化（to V，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（strong）；动词\"出台\"→roll out；词性转换（\"保障\"→protection）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"出台一系列政策\"对应官方短语见 China Daily（roll out a set of measures），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "尊老是社会和谐与发展的重要基石，有助于培育良好的社会氛围，推动整个社会文明进步。", "en": "Respecting the elderly is an important cornerstone of social harmony and development, helping to foster a sound social atmosphere and advance the progress of civilization across society.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "尊老", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"尊老是…重要基石\"/②\"有助于培育…社会氛围\"/③\"推动…社会文明进步\";核心=①;②③→分词状语(helping to…)(CL-03);话题:动名词主语(→Respecting the elderly)(CL-02);触发[有助于]→contribute to|help to V;文化:社会和谐→S1 social harmony(新华英版)", "ta": "动名词主语；分词状语（helping to…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（sound / across society）；词性转换（\"文明进步\"→the progress of civilization）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"社会和谐\"对应官方短语见新华英版（social harmony），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "节俭 (frugality) 是中华民族的传统美德。", "en": "Frugality is a traditional virtue of the Chinese nation.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "节俭", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→Frugality);文化:节俭→frugality(原卷括注，同源优先)|thriftiness(教材术语库 Ch1，异译)；中华民族→S1 the Chinese nation；传统美德→traditional virtues(S3)", "ta": "补主语（Frugality，Li & Thompson 1976）；意合→形合。", "te": "增补（the）；词性转换（\"传统美德\"→a traditional virtue）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"节俭\"从原卷括注 frugality（同源优先）；教材术语库另作 thriftiness（异译，追加不删）；\"中华民族\"见国新办白皮书，S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国古代，生产力低下，先人们深知劳动成果来之不易，因而秉持节俭的理念。", "en": "In ancient China, productivity was low, and our forebears knew well that the fruits of labour were hard-won, so they upheld the value of frugality.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "节俭", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①②\"生产力低下/先人们深知…来之不易\"/③\"秉持节俭的理念\";核心=③;①→背景状语/原因;②→宾语从句(knew well that…);逻辑:因果→so|therefore;话题:句首框架状语(在中国古代)前置;补主语(→our forebears);时锚:古代→一般过去时", "ta": "状语前置（连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（因果 so，连淑能 1993）；补主语。", "te": "增补（well / our）；惯用等值（\"来之不易\"→hard-won）；词性转换（\"理念\"→the value of）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "到了物质极大丰富的今天，中国人民依然坚持节俭的生活方式，体现了理性消费的生活理念和对劳动的尊重。", "en": "In today's era of material abundance, the Chinese people still adhere to a frugal lifestyle, which reflects a rational approach to consumption and a respect for labour.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "节俭", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"到了…今天\"/②\"中国人民依然坚持…生活方式\"/③\"体现了…\";核心=②;①→时间状语(In today's era of material abundance);③→非限定定从(which reflects…)|分词;话题:补主语(→the Chinese people);时锚:今天/依然→一般现在时", "ta": "状语前置；补主语；意合→形合（非限定 which，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "词性转换（\"理性消费的生活理念\"→a rational approach to consumption）；增补（still）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "近年来，中国政府持续加强节约型社会的建设，倡导“光盘行动”，减少食物浪费，提倡简约低碳的生活方式，推进资源循环利用，反对过度消费。", "en": "In recent years, the Chinese government has continuously strengthened the building of a conservation-oriented society, promoting the \"Clear Your Plate\" campaign, reducing food waste, advocating a simple, low-carbon lifestyle, advancing resource recycling, and opposing excessive consumption.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "节俭", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①\"加强节约型社会的建设\"/②\"倡导光盘行动\"/③\"减少食物浪费\"/④\"提倡简约低碳的生活方式\"/⑤\"推进资源循环利用\"/⑥\"反对过度消费\";核心=①;②③④⑤⑥→并列 V-ing;时锚:近年来→现在完成时|现在完成进行时;话题:补主语(→the Chinese government);文化:光盘行动→S1 Clear Your Plate Campaign(术语库/官媒)；节约型社会→S4 a conservation-oriented society；低碳→low-carbon", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（并列 V-ing，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词并列→V-ing（增补，Molina & Albir 2002）；词性转换（\"浪费\"→waste）；惯用等值（\"光盘行动\"→Clear Your Plate campaign）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"光盘行动\"对应官方定译见术语库（Clear Your Plate Campaign），S1；原卷全角引号“光盘行动”照抄，英译以引号标注。"}, {"term": "high-quality development", "zh": "高质量发展", "cn": "节约型社会的建设有力地推动了中国经济的高质量发展。", "en": "The building of a conservation-oriented society has strongly driven the high-quality development of the Chinese economy.", "ptype": "术语", "cat": "真题B", "map": "节俭", "set": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2025·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→The building of a conservation-oriented society)|动名词主语;文化:高质量发展→S1 high-quality development(定译)；节约型社会→S4", "ta": "补主语；名词化（\"建设\"→the building of，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "副词化（\"有力\"→strongly）；词性转换（\"推动\"→has driven）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"高质量发展\"对应官方定译（新华/scio/gov，high-quality development），S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "反映在艺术和文学中的乡村生活理想是中国文明的重要特征。", "en": "The ideal of rural life reflected in art and literature is an important feature of Chinese civilization.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "乡村生活理想（儒家与道家）", "set": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:长定语前置→主语(The ideal of rural life);修饰后置(reflected in art and literature);补主语;文化:中国文明→Chinese civilization(S1 官方行文)", "ta": "修饰语后置（\"反映在…的\"→reflected in…，连淑能 1993）；补主语。", "te": "名词化（\"文明\"→civilization）；增补（the）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"中国文明/中华文明\"官方作 Chinese civilization，S1。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这在很大程度上归功于道家（Taoist) 对自然的情感。", "en": "This is to a great extent due to the Taoists' affection for nature.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "乡村生活理想（儒家与道家）", "set": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→this);文化:道家→Taoist(原卷括注，同源优先)|Daoism(教材术语库 Ch1，异译)；备注:[原卷如此]（括注\"（Taoist) \"为全角左括号＋半角右括号＋空格，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合。", "te": "名词化（\"情感\"→affection）；增补（to a great extent）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"道家\"从原卷括注 Taoist，同源优先；教材术语库另作 Daoism（异译，追加不删）。"}, {"term": "traditional Chinese painting", "zh": "中国画", "cn": "传统中国画有两个最受青睐的主题，一是家庭生活的各种幸福场景，画中往往有老人在饮茶下棋，男人在耕耘收割，妇女在织布缝衣，小孩在户外玩耍。", "en": "Traditional Chinese painting has two most favored themes. One is the various happy scenes of family life, in which there are often old people drinking tea and playing chess, men plowing and harvesting, women weaving and sewing, and children playing outdoors.", "ptype": "术语", "cat": "真题B", "map": "乡村生活理想（儒家与道家）", "set": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①/②;核心=①;②→说明/同位(there be ＋ V-ing 并列);逻辑:并列→分句|and;话题:补主语(→traditional Chinese painting);文化:中国画→traditional Chinese painting(教材术语库 Ch3)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（拆句＋in which，连淑能 1993）；非谓语并列（V-ing）；修饰语后置。", "te": "动词并列→V-ing（增补，Molina & Albir 2002）；切分长句。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"传统中国画\"对应教材定译 traditional Chinese painting（教材术语库 Ch3）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "另一个则是乡村生活的种种乐趣，画有渔夫在湖上打渔，农夫在山上欲柴采药，或是书生坐在松树下呤诗作画。", "en": "The other is the many pleasures of rural life, depicting fishermen fishing on the lake, farmers gathering firewood and herbs in the mountains, or scholars sitting under pine trees composing poems and painting.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "乡村生活理想（儒家与道家）", "set": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:分句①/②;核心=①;②→伴随状语(分词 depicting …)(CL-03);话题:补主语(→The other);文化:书生→scholar；备注:[原卷如此]（\"欲柴\"疑为\"砍柴\"、\"呤诗\"疑为\"吟诗\"，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（连淑能 1993）；非谓语并列。", "te": "动词→V-ing（增补，Molina & Albir 2002）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这两个主题可以分别代表儒家 (Confucian)和道家的生活理想。", "en": "These two themes can respectively represent the Confucian and Taoist ideals of life.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "乡村生活理想（儒家与道家）", "set": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "src": "CET6 2014·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 1", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→these two themes);文化:儒家→Confucian(原卷括注，同源优先)|Confucianism(教材术语库 Ch1，异译)；道家→Taoist(原卷括注，同源优先)；备注:[原卷如此]（\"儒家 (Confucian)\"为半角空格＋半角括号，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；修饰语后置（the Confucian and Taoist ideals of life）。", "te": "副词化（\"分别\"→respectively）；词性转换（\"生活理想\"→ideals of life）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"儒家/道家\"从原卷括注 Confucian / Taoist，同源优先；教材术语库另作 Confucianism / Daoism（异译，追加不删）。"}], "unitGk": {}, "examMatrix": [], "exercises": [{"sec": "Comprehension 理解填空", "kind": "fill", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "Philosophy in the pre-Qin period was marked by the emergence of various ancient philosophical views. The most influential schools were ______, Daoism, ______, Mohism, and ______.", "ans": "Confucianism；Legalism；Mohism", "src": "Chapter 1 · 先秦子学（Philosophy in the Pre-Qin Period）"}, {"no": 2, "q": "Ancient Chinese philosophers share some common characteristics, such as their stress on human existence, ______, ______, ______, and intuition.", "ans": "practice；morality；harmony", "src": "Chapter 1 · 古代中国哲学的基本特征（Basic Features）"}, {"no": 3, "q": "Chinese ethics and morality focus more on ______, collectivism, and ______ life.", "ans": "harmony；spiritual", "src": "Chapter 1 · 中国伦理道德的特征"}, {"no": 4, "q": "China, as a country with a rich diversity of religions, has indigenous Daoist beliefs and religions of foreign origin, such as ______ and ______.", "ans": "Buddhism；Islam", "src": "Chapter 1 · 宗教总述"}, {"no": 5, "q": "______, located to the east of Luoyang, Henan Province, was the first officially built Buddhist temple in China in the Eastern Han Dynasty.", "ans": "The White Horse Temple", "src": "Chapter 1 · 佛教寺院（The White Horse Temple 白马寺）"}, {"no": 6, "q": "Explain briefly: universal love.", "ans": "Universal love（兼爱）is a principle emphasised by Mohism. It encourages people to love others, including their families and people of other states, as they love themselves, so that all may be treated equally.", "src": "Chapter 1 · 墨家（Mohism）"}, {"no": 7, "q": "Explain briefly: non-action.", "ans": "Non-action（无为）is a basic concept of Daoism. It means letting things take their natural course and refraining from forced or unnatural interference. It emphasises respecting natural laws rather than engaging in unnecessary or excessive actions.", "src": "Chapter 1 · 道家（Daoism）"}]}, {"sec": "Translation 术语翻译", "kind": "table", "table": {"head": ["Term", "Translation"], "rows": [["罢黜百家，独尊儒术", "Rejecting the other schools of thought and respecting only Confucianism"], ["百家争鸣", "the contention of a hundred schools of thought"], ["仁、义、礼、智、信", "benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and fidelity"], ["八仙过海，各显神通", "the Eight Immortals crossing the sea, each displaying his or her magical powers"], ["天时、地利、人和", "Opportunities of time granted by Heaven, advantages of situation afforded by the Earth, and the union arising from the accord of Men"]]}, "intro": "Translate the following terms into English according to the reading text.", "src": "Chapter 1 · Translation 术语翻译"}, {"sec": "Passage Translation 段落翻译", "kind": "passage", "cn": "“知行合一”可能是王守仁哲学中最为人熟知和广泛关注的一个命题。“知是行的主意，行是知的功夫；知是行之始，行是知之成。”在王氏哲学中，人们应该寻求“知行合一”，因为行动和知识就像一枚硬币的两面，两者不可分割、同时发生。除非将知识与行动结合起来，否则无法获得真正的知识。这一思想告诉人们，仅仅学习理论知识是远远不够的。要真正把握其内涵，需要付诸具体的行动。", "ans": "The “unity of knowledge and action” (Zhi Xing He Yi) is perhaps the most familiar and widely discussed proposition in Wang Shouren's philosophy. “Knowledge is the guide of action, and action is the practice of knowledge; knowledge is the beginning of action, and action is the completion of knowledge.” In Wang's philosophy, people should seek the unity of knowledge and action, because action and knowledge are like two sides of the same coin — inseparable and simultaneous. Unless knowledge is combined with action, true knowledge cannot be attained. This idea tells people that merely learning theoretical knowledge is far from enough. To truly grasp its meaning, one must put it into concrete action.", "src": "Chapter 1 · Passage Translation 段落翻译"}, {"sec": "Critical Thinking and Discussion 思辨与讨论", "kind": "open", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "The mainstream of traditional Chinese culture is Confucianism, whose core is the Golden Mean. Can you provide an example from your own life to illustrate your understanding of the concept?", "ans": "（开放题）示例：在学习与休息之间寻求平衡——既不过度用功伤身，也不放任自流；在与同学相处时取中庸之道，不偏不倚、求同存异，最终达成和谐。", "src": "Chapter 1 · Critical Thinking and Discussion"}]}, {"sec": "Communication 口语任务", "kind": "open", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "You are a member of the Classic Culture Association at your university. To celebrate Confucius' birthday, the Association has decided to hold a Confucius Cultural Festival on 28th September. Discuss the assignment with your group members and present a detailed proposal covering: 1) the purpose of the festival, 2) activities to be held, 3) desired outcomes.", "ans": "（开放题）方案示例：目的——弘扬儒家文化；活动——经典诵读、书法展、孔子生平讲座、传统礼仪体验；成效——增进同学对传统文化的了解与认同。", "src": "Chapter 1 · Communication 口语任务"}]}], "answerSrc": [["Comprehension · Q1", "Confucianism / Legalism / Mohism"], ["Translation · 罢黜百家，独尊儒术", "Rejecting the other schools of thought and respecting only Confucianism"]], "chunkHL": [{"term": "chinese people", "zh": "中国人", "key": "chinese_people", "cnt": 24, "q": "Pu'er tea is deeply loved by Chinese people."}, {"term": "chinese culture", "zh": "中国文化", "key": "chinese_culture", "cnt": 20, "q": "It has gradually evolved into a unique element of Chinese culture."}, {"term": "silk road", "zh": "丝绸之路", "key": "silk_road", "cnt": 16, "q": "The world-famous Silk Road was a series of routes connecting the East and the West."}, {"term": "traditional chinese culture", "zh": "中国传统文化", "key": "traditional_chinese_culture", "cnt": 11, "q": "The symbolic meaning of fish is said to originate from traditional Chinese culture."}, {"term": "one of the most", "zh": "最……之一", "key": "one_of_the_most", "cnt": 11, "q": "Today, tea is one of the most popular beverages in the world."}, {"term": "great importance", "zh": "高度重视", "key": "great_importance", "cnt": 10, "q": "Chinese families attach great importance to their children's education."}, {"term": "han dynasty", "zh": "汉朝", "key": "han_dynasty", "cnt": 9, "q": "The Han Dynasty is one of the most important dynasties in Chinese history."}, {"term": "according to", "zh": "根据", "key": "according_to", "cnt": 7, "q": "According to records, a total of 72 emperors once visited the mountain."}, {"term": "ming dynasty", "zh": "明朝", "key": "ming_dynasty", "cnt": 6, "q": "Beijing Roast Duck dates back to the Ming Dynasty 600 years ago."}, {"term": "as well as", "zh": "以及", "key": "as_well_as", "cnt": 5, "q": "Meanwhile, he also introduced the customs, geography and culture as well as unique species of the Western Regions to the Central Plains, greatly broadening people's horizons."}, {"term": "thousands of years", "zh": "数千年", "key": "thousands_of_years", "cnt": 4, "q": "For thousands of years, the father's surname has been passed down from generation to generation."}, {"term": "thousand years", "zh": "千年", "key": "thousand_years", "cnt": 4, "q": "It is said that villagers along the Chishui River began to brew Moutai four thousand years ago."}, {"term": "across the country", "zh": "全国各地", "key": "across_the_country", "cnt": 4, "q": "Tea plantations spread across the country, and tea merchants grew rich."}, {"term": "in order to", "zh": "为了", "key": "in_order_to", "cnt": 4, "q": "It was in order to search for the body of Qu Yuan, the patriotic poet of the State of Chu."}, {"term": "due to", "zh": "由于", "key": "due_to", "cnt": 4, "q": "This is to a great extent due to the Taoists' affection for nature."}, {"term": "in terms of", "zh": "就……而言", "key": "in_terms_of", "cnt": 3, "q": "In terms of overall economic strength, Shenzhen ranks among China's top cities."}]}